---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import MadeByKaj from '../../../components/MadeByKaj.astro';
import LiveCodePlayground from '../../../components/live/LiveCodePlayground.astro';
import CodeBlockWithCopy from '../../../components/live/CodeBlockWithCopy.astro';
import addObjectHtml from '../../../live-examples/skykit/add-an-object/index.html?raw';

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const installCode = `npm install @found-in-space/skykit@0.2.0-alpha.1 three`;
const importCode = `import * as THREE from 'three';
import { createSkykitBrowser } from '@found-in-space/skykit/browser';
import { createObject3dPlugin } from '@found-in-space/skykit';`;
---

<Layout
	title="Add your own object to space | Found in Space"
	description="Use the beginner SkyKit browser helper to add an app-owned Three.js object to a star viewer."
>
	<SiteHeader section="Learn & build" />

	<main class="page page--lesson">
		<header class="lesson-hero">
			<nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
				<a href={`${basePath}learn-build/`}>Learn &amp; Build</a>
				<span class="breadcrumb__sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
				<a href={`${basePath}learn-build/skykit/`}>SkyKit</a>
				<span class="breadcrumb__sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
				<span>Add an object</span>
			</nav>
			<p class="eyebrow">Lesson 2</p>
			<h1>Add your own object to space</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				Create the default star viewer, add an ordinary Three.js marker, animate it, and
				toggle it with a checkbox.
			</p>
		</header>

		<article class="lesson-body prose">
			<section>
				<h2>Learning goal</h2>
				<p>
					Lesson 1 used a pasteable embed. This lesson moves one step up: you call
					<code>createSkykitBrowser()</code> so the viewer is still built for you, but your
					app gets to add a thing to the scene.
				</p>
				<p>
					The custom object is ordinary Three.js. SkyKit gives it a place among the stars;
					your app owns the shape, color, animation, and checkbox.
				</p>
			</section>
		</article>

		<LiveCodePlayground
			id="skykit-add-an-object"
			title="Add a Hyades marker"
			description="Toggle the marker, then edit its color, size, target, or pulse speed."
			initialCode={addObjectHtml}
			mode="html"
			height="34rem"
		/>

		<article class="lesson-body prose">
			<section class="callout panel">
				<p class="panel__eyebrow">Things to try</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Change the marker color.</li>
					<li>Change the marker size.</li>
					<li>Move the marker by editing <code>HYADES_CENTER_PC</code>.</li>
					<li>Add a second marker with another <code>createObject3dPlugin()</code>.</li>
					<li>Change the pulse speed in the <code>update</code> function.</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>What changed in the code</h2>
				<p>
					Lesson 1 used the pasteable embed. This lesson moves one step up:
					<code>createSkykitBrowser()</code> still builds the star viewer for us, but now we
					pass in our own app-owned object.
				</p>
				<p>
					The object is ordinary Three.js. SkyKit does not own its shape, color, animation,
					or checkbox. SkyKit only gives it a place in the sky scene.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="callout panel">
				<p class="panel__eyebrow">Under the hood</p>
				<p>
					The marker position line converts Hyades parsec coordinates into the viewer's
					default scene scale. <code>createObject3dPlugin()</code> then places that ordinary
					Three.js object in the star scene.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="source-panel panel">
				<p class="panel__eyebrow">Source and next steps</p>
				<h2>Use package examples for larger object layers.</h2>
				<p>
					This live example is intentionally small. The SkyKit custom object example shows
					the same idea with constellation art, a guide sphere, and a Hyades annotation.
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li><a href="https://github.com/Found-in-Space/skykit" rel="noopener noreferrer">SkyKit repository</a></li>
					<li><a href="https://github.com/Found-in-Space/skykit/tree/main/packages/skykit/examples/custom-object-layer" rel="noopener noreferrer">Custom object layer example</a></li>
					<li><a href={`${basePath}learn/topic/radio-bubble/`}>Found in Space radio bubble lesson</a></li>
					<li><a href={`${basePath}learn-build/skykit/query-stars-without-three/`}>Next: query stars without Three.js</a></li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section class="local-panel panel">
				<h2>Use this locally</h2>
				<p>
					In a local app, install SkyKit and Three.js. The object itself is ordinary
					Three.js, so your app can decide how to generate, animate, and dispose it.
				</p>
				<CodeBlockWithCopy code={installCode} label="Install" language="bash" />
				<CodeBlockWithCopy code={importCode} label="Local imports" language="js" />
			</section>
		</article>

		<MadeByKaj />
	</main>
</Layout>

<style>
	.page--lesson {
		max-width: 76rem;
	}

	.lesson-hero {
		max-width: 60rem;
		padding: 3.5rem 0 1.5rem;
	}

	.lesson-hero h1 {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 4rem;
		font-weight: 600;
		line-height: 1;
		letter-spacing: 0;
	}

	.lesson-body {
		max-width: 54rem;
	}

	.source-panel {
		margin: 2.5rem 0;
	}

	.source-panel h2 {
		margin-top: 0;
	}

	.local-panel {
		margin: 2.5rem 0;
	}

	.local-panel h2 {
		margin-top: 0;
	}

	@media (max-width: 720px) {
		.lesson-hero h1 {
			font-size: 2.75rem;
		}
	}
</style>
